# M3: explicit presence, as three cases and not one column
#   design/SHAPES.md: a by-value group reports absent and empty identically
#   unless it is designed not to. These are the three rows that say whether it
#   was. 200 elements; presence cycles per field and one element in seven is
#   present AND zero.

field                arm                absent   present+zero   present+nonzero  total
opt_count (int32)    armonik                67             19               114    200
opt_label (string)   armonik                50             21               129    200
opt_flag  (bool)     armonik                40             92                68    200
opt_count (int32)    core-native            67             19               114    200
opt_label (string)   core-native            50             21               129    200
opt_flag  (bool)     core-native            40             92                68    200
opt_count (int32)    core-ffi-rust          67             19               114    200
opt_label (string)   core-ffi-rust          50             21               129    200
opt_flag  (bool)     core-ffi-rust          40             92                68    200
opt_count (int32)    prost                  67             19               114    200
opt_label (string)   prost                  50             21               129    200
opt_flag  (bool)     prost                  40             92                68    200

#   All three cases occur on every field, so the shape is exercised and not
#   merely present. present+zero is the one a group cannot tell from absent
#   unless its presence WORD carries it, which is what makes these rows a test.

# M3: the oneof, by member

member                         count
as_int (int64)                    40
as_text (string)                  40
as_blob (bytes)                   40
as_stamp (message)                40
as_nothing (payload-free)         40
none set                           0

#   `as_nothing` is the payload-free member: present, empty, and selected by
#   its presence alone. It is 40 of 200 here, so it is reached.

# Fields the reader does not know (README section 10 item 1)
#   Hand-built wire. No manifest hash: a corpus generated from the schema that
#   reads it cannot contain one of these. The check is that all four arms agree
#   on the decoded value and on the re-encoded bytes.

vector                                       arms agree   re-encode    unknown survives
unknown oneof tag 15 AFTER a known member    ok           ok           no: the oneof stays at as_int
unknown oneof tag 15 BEFORE a known member   ok           ok           no: the oneof stays at as_int
unknown oneof tag 15 ALONE                   ok           ok           no: the oneof is None
unknown fields, all four wire types, top level ok           ok           no
unknown field inside a nested message        ok           ok           no
unknown field BEFORE every known field       ok           ok           no
ResultRaw.status = 999 (unknown enum value)  ok           ok           YES: round-trips as Unknown(999)

# M4: the adapter site. One facade type, two wire forms.
#   Checked by state and not by payload: P4.1's `error` is a sentence in all
#   200 elements, so the payload exercises the ONE case that works. The states
#   where the map is not injective never occur in it.

state              -> nested wire             nested round trip    -> plain wire              plain round trip
Invalid            <field absent>             ok                   ""                         ok
Ok                 success=true, error=""     ok                   ""                         LOSES -> Invalid
Error("boom")      success=false, error="boom" ok                   "boom"                     ok

#   The nested form round-trips every state. The PLAIN form cannot: `Ok` and
#   `Invalid` both encode to the empty string, so one of them must come back
#   wrong whatever the adapter author chooses. This one returns `Invalid`,
#   which loses `Ok`; the intuitive alternative returns `Ok`, which silently
#   claims success for a task that reported no outcome at all. That is the
#   defect design/SHAPES.md says only a byte corpus catches, and the corpus
#   as it stands does not: no payload reaches either state.

#   The nested site has a worse problem, reported and not fixed: the payload
#   generator fills TaskOutput.success and TaskOutput.error INDEPENDENTLY, so
#   P2.x contains `success=true, error=<sentence>` -- a state no adapter over
#   {Ok, Error(d)} can represent at all. Counted over 200 elements the only
#   combinations present are (true, non-empty) and (false, non-empty); the
#   success state (true, empty) never occurs.

# M4: are the adapter's three states reachable from the payload set now?
#   Counted off DECODED values, not from the builder, and the state is read the
#   way an adapter would read it rather than from the fields directly.

  nested site (TaskDetailed.output, P2.2, 500 elements)
      Ok       (success, no error) 167
      Error    (error, no success) 167
      Invalid  (no child at all)   166
      IMPOSSIBLE (success AND error, which the message's own comment forbids) 0

  plain site (TaskSummary.error, P4.1, 200 elements)
      Error        (non-empty string)              67
      Ok OR Invalid (empty string -- INDISTINGUISHABLE) 133

#   The plain site's second row IS the finding: 133 elements carry a state the
#   wire form cannot tell apart, so an adapter must return one of two answers for
#   all of them and be wrong on the other. Before this the row was 0 and the
#   defect was unreachable.

VERDICT: every shape above is exercised and every arm agrees.
